DataBricks

    Backstory why DataBricks came into picture
        WHY?
        Data engineer were using - Apache Spark to process the data.
        Data Scientists were using - Jupyter Notebook to build Machine Learning Models
        Data Analysts were using - SQL for their business Intelligence
        and
        ML Ops teams were using separate platform for model deployment.

        This created what we called Data Silos
        Data Silos:
            Data Silos are the collections of data held in separate systems or departments within an organisation,
            making that data inaccessible or isolated from other parts of the business.

        Imagine you are a data scientists working on a ML model, now you wants to deploy your model into production,
        but wait data engineers are using completely different tools to process the data and ML engineers are using
        completely different tools to deploy the models, and data analysts can't even access the data.
        This back and forth use to take weeks and even months, basically companies are spending more time on
        infra/management rather than an actually taking the insights and decisions.
        There was a major gap, studies shows that 87% of ML projects never made it to productions.

        So the Industries need unified platform for Data Engineers, Data Scientists, Data Analysts and ML Engineers.
        This is where the DataBricks came into the picture..

    |

    built on top of Apache Spark to provide cloud based, collaborative environment to handle all those things.

    What is DataBricks?
        It's a unified analytics platform that combines data engineering, data science, ML, and analytics at once place.

    Architecture of DataBricks:
        At the highest level databricks has 2 components
        1. Control Plane
        2. Compute Plane

        1. Control Plane:
            Place where data bricks manages all of the backend service. It's the things that you don't have to worry
            about, data bricks has taken care off.

            for example:
            a. The web application that we interact with
            b. User management
            c. Cluster orchestration
            d. Job scheduling

            Think like CONTROL PLANE is the brain of databricks.
            This run on databricks account itself so we don't have to worry about.
            So the CONTROL PLANE is more about backend services or things that we interact with.

        2. Compute Plane:
            This is the area where the data is actually processed.

            There are 2 types of COMPUTE PLANE
            a. Serverless compute plane
                In this databricks manages compute in their own account. So we just focus on writing the code and
                getting the work done, and don't need to worry about scaling or Infra anything.
            b. Classic Compute Plane
                So all of the compute will run in our own personal AWS or Azure account. We have to manage all the
                network and Infra from outside.

